S|       28      EXT. CAVE ENTRANCE/DIVERS CAMP - DAY                    28           
O|
Y|               DAY 4 - Tuesday June 26                                              
O|
N|               The rain streams down.                                               
O|
N|               A shelter has been erected across from the cave entrance, and        
N|               here the PARENTS of the boys are huddled on plastic chairs,          
N|               trying to sleep as the rain streams down. Lek lies curled up         
N|               asleep on the ground. Buahom can’t sleep. She sits apart from        
N|               the others, gazing towards the bright light spilling from the        
N|               cave.                                                                
N|               Park Ranger Saisor comes to the families camp carrying half a        
N|               dozen clear plastic bags. He lays them out on a table. The           
N|               parents gather round.                                                
O|
C|                                   SAISOR                                           
U|                         We found these when we went in on                          
U|                         the first night.                                           
O|
N|               The parents look more closely. Then they start to recognize          
N|               items, and to retrieve them: a pair of black sneakers, many          
N|               pairs of slip-on sandals, half a dozen backpacks. Buahom             
N|               finds her son’s sandals.                                             
N|               Great trucks are now pulling into the muddy space by the             
N|               cave. Teams of WATER ENGINEERS move GIANT PUMPS into                 
N|               position, and lay lines of giant hose. Among them a big              
N,I|               cheerful water engineer called UDOM.                                 
O|
N|               Engines start to rumble, and the water starts to flow through        
N|               the hoses in torrents, and gush out into open ground. More           
N|               news crews are arriving and setting up their cameras.                
O|
N|               A convoy of official cars pulls in to the muddy rain-drenched        
N|               site. Buahom follows the cars’ arrival as if they might be           
N|               bringing a savior. Governor Narongsak and Preeya head for the        
N|               cars to greet the VIP. As they go, the Governor’s attention          
N,I|               is caught by a slight boyish ginger-haired figure: THANET            
N,I|               NATISRI, an Americanised Thai. He’s photographing the pumps          
N|               in action with his iPad.                                             
C|                                   NARONGSAK                                        
U|                         Who’s that?                                                
O|
C|                                   PREEYA                                           
U|                         I don’t know, sir.                                         
O|
O|
